{{also|bücket|Bucket}}
==English==
[[Image:Balde.PNG|thumb|A plastic bucket]]
[[File:Caterpillar 330B Hydraulic Excavator bucket - Hillsboro, Oregon.JPG|thumb|An excavator bucket]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|buket}}, {{m|enm|boket}}, partly from {{der|en|ang|bucc}} {{gloss|"bucket, pitcher"; mod. dialectal {{m|en|buck}}}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|bouk|et}}; and partly from {{der|en|xno|buket}}, {{m|fro|buquet|t=tub; pail}} (compare {{cog|nrf|boutchet}}, {{cog|nrf|bouquet}}), diminutive of {{der|en|fro|buc|t=abdomen; object with a cavity}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*būcus}} (compare {{cog|oc|-}} and {{cog|ca|buc}}, {{m+|it|buco}}, {{m|it|buca||hole, gap}}), from {{der|en|frk|*būk|t=belly, stomach}}. Both the Old English and Frankish terms derive from {{der|en|gem-pro|*būkaz||belly, stomach}}. More at {{l|en|bouk}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|bûk'ĭt}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈbʌkɪt/}}
* {{a|GA|weak vowel}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbʌkət/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-bucket.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌkɪt|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|buck|et}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[container]] made of [[rigid]] material, often with a [[handle]], used to [[carry]] liquids or small items.
#: {{synonyms|en|pail}}
#: {{ux|en|I need a '''bucket''' to carry the water from the well.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1922|author=w:Virginia Woolf|title=w:Jacob's Room|chapter=1|passage=The crab was cool and very light. But the water was thick with sand, and so, scrambling down, Jacob was about to jump, holding his '''bucket''' in front of him, when he saw, stretched entirely rigid, side by side, their faces very red, an enormous man and woman.}}
# The [[amount]] held in this container.
#: {{ux|en|The horse drank a whole '''bucket''' of water.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly|in the plural}} A large amount of liquid.
#: {{ux|en|It rained '''buckets''' yesterday.}}
#: {{ux|en|I was so nervous that I sweated '''buckets'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly|in the plural}} A great deal of anything.
#: {{ux|en|My new suit cost me '''buckets'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We had '''buckets''' of fun.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|archaic}} A unit of measure equal to four [[gallon]]s.
# Part of a [[piece]] of [[machinery]] that [[resemble]]s a bucket (container).
#: {{synonyms|en|scoop|vane|blade}}
# {{lb|en|MTE|slang}} an insult term used in [[Toronto]] to refer to someone who habitually uses crack cocaine.
# {{lb|en|slang}} An old [[vehicle]] that is not in good working [[order]].
#: {{syn|en|banger|beater|hooptie|jalopy|wreck|crock|shitbox|rustbucket|Thesaurus:old car}}
# {{lb|en|basketball|informal}} The [[basket]].
#: {{ux|en|The forward drove to the '''bucket'''.}}
# {{lb|en|basketball|informal}} A [[field]] [[goal]].
#: {{ux|en|We can't keep giving up easy '''buckets'''.}}
# {{lb|en|variation management}} A mechanism for avoiding the [[allocation]] of targets in cases of [[mismanagement]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} A storage space in a [[hash table]] for every item sharing a particular [[key]].
# {{lb|en|aviation|mechanical engineering|uncommon}} A [[turbine]] [[blade]] driven by hot [[gas]] or [[steam]].
# A [[bucket bag]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1989|author=Susan Ludwig; Janice Steinberg|title=Petite Style|page=46
|passage=Avoid bulky styles such as duffle sacks, '''buckets''', doctors' satchels, and hobos.}}
# The [[leather]] [[socket]] for holding the whip when driving, or for the carbine or lance when mounted.
# The [[pitcher]] in certain [[orchid]]s.
# {{lb|en|slang|humorous}} A [[helmet]].

